                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Replaces each value of an array with the running product up to and including it: the first value, then the first two multiplied, then the first three, and so on.\n\nIt is cumsum with multiplying instead of adding. Use it for anything that compounds: growth by a rate per step (interest, a population, a pitch rising by a fixed ratio per note), decay by a factor per step (an echo fading), or a chain of gains or scale factors applied one after another.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cumprod <axis: int>\nDefault axis -1, the last axis: along each row.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.cumprod\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is multiplied out and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. A single number has no axis to run along, and sends nothing.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 runs along each row on its own, 0 or -2 runs down each column. Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have sends nothing. Changing it does not send by itself; the next array uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncumprod:\nThe running products, the same shape as the input. A single 0 anywhere makes every value after it 0. Whole numbers stay whole numbers (int64), which silently overflow past about 9 x 10 to the 18th - beyond 20 factorial.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: make_ones sends 40 ones ten times a second, and * turns them into forty copies of the growth rate, 1.05. t array array sends those to pack 2 first (its right outlet), then to np.cumprod, whose running product completes the pack. The plot shows the flat input line and its cumprod: 1.05, then 1.05 times 1.05, ... - compound growth, a curve that bends upward ever faster and reaches about 7 after 40 steps. Change the * value on the node: 1.02 grows slowly, 0.95 decays towards 0, 1 stays flat. Above about 1.055 the curve runs off the top of the plot (0 to 8).\n\nBottom: 1 2 3 4 5 6 multiplied out gives 1 2 6 24 120 720 - the factorials.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum adds instead of multiplying. np.diff is the reverse of cumsum, not of cumprod (for cumprod, divide each value by the one before). t.cumprod is the PyTorch version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 900,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Replaces each value of an array with the running product up to and including it: the first value, then the first two multiplied, then the first three, and so on.\n\nIt is cumsum with multiplying instead of adding. Use it for anything that compounds: growth by a rate per step (interest, a population, a pitch rising by a fixed ratio per note), decay by a factor per step (an echo fading), or a chain of gains or scale factors applied one after another.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cumprod <axis: int>\nDefault axis -1, the last axis: along each row.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.cumprod\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is multiplied out and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. A single number has no axis to run along, and sends nothing.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 runs along each row on its own, 0 or -2 runs down each column. Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have sends nothing. Changing it does not send by itself; the next array uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncumprod:\nThe running products, the same shape as the input. A single 0 anywhere makes every value after it 0. Whole numbers stay whole numbers (int64), which silently overflow past about 9 x 10 to the 18th - beyond 20 factorial.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: make_ones sends 40 ones ten times a second, and * turns them into forty copies of the growth rate, 1.05. t array array sends those to pack 2 first (its right outlet), then to np.cumprod, whose running product completes the pack. The plot shows the flat input line and its cumprod: 1.05, then 1.05 times 1.05, ... - compound growth, a curve that bends upward ever faster and reaches about 7 after 40 steps. Change the * value on the node: 1.02 grows slowly, 0.95 decays towards 0, 1 stays flat. Above about 1.055 the curve runs off the top of the plot (0 to 8).\n\nBottom: 1 2 3 4 5 6 multiplied out gives 1 2 6 24 120 720 - the factorials.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum adds instead of multiplying. np.diff is the reverse of cumsum, not of cumprod (for cumprod, divide each value by the one before). t.cumprod is the PyTorch version."
                },
